---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../components/SiteHeader.astro';
import CaseStudyNav from '../../components/CaseStudyNav.astro';
import journeyMap1 from '../../assets/images/journey-map-1.png';
import journeyMap2 from '../../assets/images/journey-map-2.png';
import raaJourney from '../../assets/images/raa-journey.png';
import raaPersona1 from '../../assets/images/raa-persona.png';
import raaPersona2 from '../../assets/images/detailed-donald.png';
import raaPersona3 from '../../assets/images/quick-quote-quinn.png';
import journeyDirectory from '../../assets/images/raa-journey-directory.png';
---
<Layout title="Journey Mapping | Andrew Smith" description="Journey mapping as a practice of shared visibility — connecting analytics, research, and team knowledge into a single reference that keeps decisions grounded in evidence.">
  <a class="skip-link" href="#main-content">Skip to content</a>
  <div class="case-page">
    <SiteHeader />
    <main id="main-content">
      <section class="case-hero">
        <p class="eyebrow"><a class="breadcrumb-link" href="/portfolio/work">Work</a><span class="breadcrumb-separator">/</span><span>Journey Mapping</span></p>
        <h1 class="display-title">Journey mapping for experience visibility.</h1>
        <p class="hero-text">Most organisations have more data about their customers than they can make sense of. Analytics in one platform, research findings in another, customer feedback somewhere else, and no shared view connecting any of it to the specific moment in the product where the experience is breaking down.</p>
        <p class="hero-text">A well-maintained journey map changes that. It gives product, engineering, service design, and UX a single shared reference — so when decisions get made about what to build next, they are grounded in what is actually known about the experience rather than what was most recently discussed in a meeting.</p>
        <p class="hero-subtext">Used across insurance, travel, membership, and identity products.</p>
      </section>
      <section class="meta-strip">
        <div class="meta-item"><p class="meta-label">Scope</p><p class="meta-value">Insurance, membership, travel, identity</p></div>
        <div class="meta-item"><p class="meta-label">Role</p><p class="meta-value">Senior UX Design Lead</p></div>
        <div class="meta-item"><p class="meta-label">Tools</p><p class="meta-value">Journey management platform, Miro, FigJam</p></div>
      </section>

      <section class="lead-image-section">
        <Image class="lead-image" src={journeyMap1} alt="Journey explorer showing phases, win rates, drop-offs, emotion curve, and prioritised solutions with member and business value ratings" decoding="async" widths={[720,1080,1440]} sizes="(max-width:78rem) 100vw, 78rem"/>
      </section>

      <section class="summary-section">
        <p class="section-kicker">Why Journey Management</p>
        <h2>The loop that connects measurement to action.</h2>
        <div class="summary-main">
          <p>The value of journey mapping is not the map itself — it is what happens when it stays connected to real data and real decisions. <a href="/portfolio/work/research-benchmarking">Benchmarking</a> feeds quantitative metrics into the map at each stage. Research findings tag pain points to specific steps. Analytics gaps surface where the team is making decisions without adequate evidence. The map becomes a living reference rather than a document produced for a presentation and filed away.</p>
          <p>That connection changes how workshops run. When product, engineering, and experience design teams arrive at an ideation session with a shared map that already captures what is known about the experience, the time goes toward genuine unknowns rather than re-establishing context that already exists. A smaller team was able to contribute more meaningfully to cross-squad work because the knowledge was accessible rather than locked in individual heads or buried in folders.</p>
          <p>Solutions get prioritised by member value and business value, tagged to the specific stage of the journey where the problem lives, and tracked against the roadmap. When a metric shifts, the map shows exactly which stage is affected and what the current prioritised responses are — so the connection between measurement and action is visible, not inferred.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="editorial-section">
        <div class="section-rail"><p class="section-kicker">Metrics-Connected Maps</p></div>
        <div class="story-block">
          <p>The journey explorer below connects real analytics to each stage of the experience — win rates, drop-off rates, failure rates, and emotion scores. Solutions are prioritised by member value and business value, and the map shows which ones are on the roadmap, in delivery, or on hold.</p>
          <p>This is what a journey map looks like when it is actually used by product and engineering teams alongside experience design — not a static artefact, but a shared decision-making tool that evolves as the product does.</p>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section class="supporting-image-section">
        <Image class="supporting-image" src={journeyMap2} alt="Journey explorer with solution detail panel open showing priority, effort, member value, and business value" loading="lazy" decoding="async" widths={[720,1080,1440]} sizes="(max-width:78rem) 100vw, 78rem"/>
      </section>

      <section class="editorial-section">
        <div class="section-rail"><p class="section-kicker">Research-Grounded Maps</p></div>
        <div class="story-block">
          <p>Before digital journey management tools, maps were built from research synthesis — combining interviews, usability sessions, analytics, and team knowledge into a shared artefact. The map below shows a full member experience from initial awareness through to post-booking, with emotional states, touchpoints, pain points, and opportunities at each stage.</p>
          <p>Maps built this way serve two purposes. They align the team around what the experience actually is, not what it was designed to be. And they surface knowledge gaps — the stages where the team is making decisions without adequate research, and where investment in discovery would have the greatest impact. Those gaps feed directly into the <a href="/portfolio/work/ux-process">discovery process</a> as priority items.</p>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section class="supporting-image-section">
        <Image class="supporting-image" src={raaJourney} alt="Journey map showing member stages from research through booking with moods, touchpoints, pain points, and opportunities" loading="lazy" decoding="async" widths={[720,1080,1440]} sizes="(max-width:78rem) 100vw, 78rem"/>
      </section>

      <section class="editorial-section">
        <div class="section-rail"><p class="section-kicker">Journey Map Directory</p></div>
        <div class="story-block">
          <p>Journey maps across all RAA product areas are maintained in a shared directory — Road Service, Car Insurance, Home Insurance, Membership, and Solar and Energy. Each map is linked and tagged by lifecycle stage, digital channel availability, and opportunity type, giving product and engineering teams a single navigable reference for where the experience has been mapped and where gaps still exist.</p>
          <p>The directory structure makes it possible to identify which areas have adequate research coverage and which need discovery investment before any solution work begins.</p>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section class="supporting-image-section">
        <Image class="supporting-image" src={journeyDirectory} alt="Miro journey map directory showing Road Service, Car Insurance, and Home Insurance product areas with lifecycle stages and digital channel tags" loading="lazy" decoding="async" widths={[720,1080,1440]} sizes="(max-width:78rem) 100vw, 78rem"/>
      </section>

      <section class="editorial-section">
        <div class="section-rail"><p class="section-kicker">Personas</p></div>
        <div class="story-block">
          <p>A persona grounds every design decision in a real human with real motivations and real constraints. The persona below was built from research and connected to the analytics data showing which member segments show the highest drop-off at the research and inspiration stage — so it is not a demographic average but a representation of a real pattern in real behaviour.</p>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section class="supporting-image-section">
        <Image class="supporting-image" src={raaPersona} alt="User persona with background, travel goals, research behaviour, likes, dislikes, and device usage" loading="lazy" decoding="async" widths={[720,1080,1440]} sizes="(max-width:78rem) 100vw, 78rem"/>
      </section>

      <div class="image-scroll-track-wrap">
        <div class="image-scroll-track">
          <figure class="scroll-fig">
            <Image class="scroll-img" src={raaPersona1} alt="RAA member persona showing background, travel goals, research behaviour, and device usage" loading="lazy" decoding="async" widths={[420,600]} sizes="420px"/>
            <figcaption>Member persona — grounded in research and connected to analytics showing where members drop off in the experience</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure class="scroll-fig">
            <Image class="scroll-img" src={raaPersona3} alt="Quick Quote Quinn persona showing goals, needs, pain points and expectations across the quote journey" loading="lazy" decoding="async" widths={[420,600]} sizes="420px"/>
            <figcaption>Quick Quote Quinn — wants a fast competitive quote without jargon. Will compare insurers but trusts RAA. Drops off if questions are too many or too complex.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure class="scroll-fig">
            <Image class="scroll-img" src={raaPersona2} alt="Detailed Donald persona showing goals, needs, pain points and expectations for a confident considered purchase" loading="lazy" decoding="async" widths={[420,600]} sizes="420px"/>
            <figcaption>Detailed Donald — confident but cautious. Wants clear cover inclusions, easy access to PDS, and painless member pricing. Needs help content for complex parts without it getting in the way.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
      </div>

      <CaseStudyNav />
    </main>
  </div>
</Layout>
<style>
.case-page{position:relative;width:min(calc(100% - 3rem),78rem);margin:0 auto;padding:1.25rem 0 5rem}
.eyebrow{font-family:var(--font-mono);font-size:.82rem;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--muted-foreground);margin:0 0 1.25rem;display:flex;gap:.5rem;align-items:center}
.breadcrumb-link{font-weight:600}.breadcrumb-separator{color:var(--muted-foreground)}
h1,h2{margin:0;font-weight:800;letter-spacing:-.05em;color:var(--foreground)}
.display-title{font-size:clamp(2rem,4.5vw,3.5rem);line-height:1.06;margin:1rem 0 1.5rem;max-width:28ch}
.hero-text,.story-block p,.summary-main p{font-size:1.08rem;line-height:1.7;margin:0 0 1rem}
.hero-subtext{font-size:1.05rem;line-height:1.65;color:var(--muted-foreground);font-style:italic;margin:.25rem 0 0;max-width:42rem}
.case-hero{padding:5rem 0 2rem}
.meta-strip{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,minmax(0,1fr));gap:2rem;padding:1.5rem 0 .5rem;border-top:1px solid var(--border)}
.meta-label{font-family:var(--font-mono);font-size:.78rem;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.06em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--muted-foreground);margin:0}
.meta-value{margin:.35rem 0 0;font-size:1.1rem;font-weight:700}
.lead-image-section{padding:2rem 0 4rem}
.lead-image,.supporting-image{width:100%;border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:1.5rem;box-shadow:var(--shadow-large)}
.supporting-image-section{padding:6rem 0 6rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--border)}
.summary-section{padding:0 0 6rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--border)}
.summary-section h2{margin-top:.75rem;font-size:clamp(1.5rem,2.5vw,2.2rem);max-width:32ch}
.summary-main{margin-top:1.5rem;max-width:44rem}
.summary-main a,.story-block a{color:var(--foreground);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:3px;text-decoration-color:var(--border)}
.summary-main a:hover,.story-block a:hover{text-decoration-color:var(--foreground)}
.section-kicker{font-family:var(--font-mono);font-size:.78rem;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.06em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--muted-foreground);margin:0 0 .5rem}
.editorial-section{display:grid;grid-template-columns:14rem minmax(0,1fr);gap:2rem;padding:6rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid var(--border)}
.story-block{max-width:44rem}
@media(max-width:860px){.meta-strip{grid-template-columns:1fr;gap:1rem}.editorial-section{grid-template-columns:1fr}}
</style>
